{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>Rav lakh.</b> Moses said before the Holy One, blessed be He: Master of the Universe, if You have vowed and sworn that I shall not enter the land, You — release Your vow, just as You released my vows when I swore to Jethro, as Rashi explained in the section ואלה שמות. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: Moses — rav lakh, meaning: you have a rav over you who can release your vow, but I have no rav over Me and I have no one to release My vows. Another interpretation: rav lakh — with the rav of flesh and blood. 'Rav lakh, O sons of Levi' — they made him a rav; 'rav lakh.' Another interpretation: you have a rav, and who is it? Joshua, whose time has come to rule. Another interpretation: rav lakh — do not add [another] word. Moses said before the Holy One, blessed be He: Master of the Universe, You called me Your servant, as it is said: ״לא כן עבדי משה״, and to Leviathan [You called] a servant, as it is said: ״תקחנו עבד עולם״, and he entreated before You, as it is said: ״הירבה אליך תחנונים״, and You made a covenant with him and You heeded him, as it is said: ״היכרות ברית עמך תקחנו לעבד עולם״ — and I am Your servant; I entreated before You, and You said to me: ״הנה אנכי כורת ברית״ etc. — and it will not be fulfilled in me, [despite] what You say to me: 'Go up and die on the mountain.' And furthermore, You wrote in the Torah: ״ואם אמר יאמר העבד אהבתי״ etc. ״ועבדו לעולם״. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: rav lakh — your adversary has already issued a decree upon you that you shall die, and this is Adam the First, who ate from the Tree of Knowledge and caused death to all creation, and this is what Job said: ״אם יעלה לשמים שיאו״ etc. — that even if a man ascends to heaven, as Moses did, who trampled the sky with his feet like the ministering angels, and the Holy One, blessed be He, spoke to him face to face — yet when his time came to die, his wings were broken and he fell before the Angel of Death. And furthermore it is written: ״ועבד חפשי מאדוניו״ — that even if his master acquired him for a thousand zuz, when his time comes to die, he cannot say: 'You are my servant and shall not go to die.'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {0 "The recurring phrase 'רב לך' is left untranslated as it is the lemma from Deuteronomy 3:26 being interpreted throughout; the wordplay (rav = 'much/enough/teacher/adversary') cannot be captured in a single English rendering. The citation from Job (אם יעלה לשמים שיאו) appears to be from Job 20:6 with the unusual reading שיאו; the text may be corrupt or reflect a variant tradition."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:00:37.346900207Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}